<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>♨️ LIVE RELATIVE HUMIDITY</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/humidity/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261009-t01z-rh-anl-g2" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Near-surface relative humidity in percent (HRRR 2 m analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> amber arid &rarr; yellow dry &rarr; green &rarr; teal humid &rarr; blue very humid &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100</b> deep-purple saturated. Same humidity always shows the same color. Saturation percentage at current temperature — not precipitable water vapor, not dew point.</p><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m relative humidity analysis, hourly cycles<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE RELATIVE HUMIDITY</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/humidity/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261009-t01z-rh-anl-g2" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Near-surface relative humidity in percent (HRRR 2 m analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>LOWEST 0</b> amber arid &rarr; yellow dry &rarr; green &rarr; teal humid &rarr; blue very humid &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 100</b> deep-purple saturated. Same humidity always shows the same color. Saturation percentage at current temperature — not precipitable water vapor, not dew point.</p><p>Near-surface (2 m) relative humidity from the operational HRRR analysis: the percentage saturation of air with water vapor at its current temperature. High values precede fog, dew, and cloud formation; low values accompany dry air, elevated evaporation, and fire-weather concerns around the Great Lakes basin.</p><p>Humidity science (atmospheric thermodynamics) studies water vapor as the fuel of weather: relative humidity governs evaporation, condensation, cloud nucleation, and atmospheric stability. It is distinct from absolute moisture measures such as precipitable water vapor and dew point, and is reported in percent.</p><p><b>Units:</b> % (display; source % as filed)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m relative humidity analysis, hourly cycles<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
      <GroundOverlay>
        <name>♨️ LIVE RELATIVE HUMIDITY</name>
        <drawOrder>10</drawOrder>
        <Icon>
          <href>https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/humidity/current.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261009-t01z-rh-anl-g2</href>
          <refreshMode>onInterval</refreshMode>
          <refreshInterval>3600</refreshInterval>
        </Icon>
        <LatLonBox>
          <north>49.5</north>
          <south>40.5</south>
          <east>-73.5</east>
          <west>-93.0</west>
        </LatLonBox>
      </GroundOverlay>
    </Folder>
  </Document>
